2. The DB2ISAM Example Program
===========================================================================

dB2Isam, Copyright (C) ENZ EDV-Beratung GmbH, 1993
B-Tree Filer Utility

DB2ISAM is a demo program that demonstrates the use of the DBIMPEXP unit.
It converts a dBase III (or dBase IV) DBF data file, and if one is present
the attached DBT memo file, to a B-Tree Filer fileblock format.

The dBase file can contain any number of memo fields of any size; however,
the final size of the Filer record cannot be greater than 64K. If required,
a Pascal include file can also be generated at the time of the conversion.


Calling Syntax
--------------

Call DB2ISAM with a command in the following syntax. (Calling the program
with no parameters or with an invalid parameter will result in a display
of DB2ISAM's calling syntax.)

Syntax:  DB2ISAM /D <dBase_filename> [/I <Filer_filename>]
                 {/<option>(+|-|n)}

  <dBase_Filename>   name for *.DBF, *.DBT and *.DMP files
  <Filer_Filename>   name for *.DAT and *.INC files
  M512               maximum size of a Memo field (0 <= n <= 65000)
  S+                 convert dBase character fields to Pascal strings
  T+                 create a Pascal include file
  A-                 insert an auto-incrementing field into the record

A parameter always begins with a slash (/) or minus character (-).

D     The name of the dBase file family is expected after this parameter.
      It will define the full data file name (by suffixing with '.DBF') and
      the memo file (by suffixing with '.DBT'). Having a space between the
      /D and the name is optional. If one or more data conversion errors
      occur, a conversion error log file (with extension '.DMP') will be
      produced with details about the errors (basically the record number
      and the field name). This parameter is required.

I     The name for the B-Tree Filer compatible data file and, if required,
      the include file, is expected after this parameter. If there is no
      /I parameter is given the dBase file family name is used. The data
      file is created with extension '.DAT' and the include file with
      '.INC'.

      If the Filer data file exists already, the user is prompted whether
      to delete it:

      D:\ISAM\MYX\EXAMPLE.DAT already exists! Overwrite it? (Y/N) :_

      If the response is 'N' then the conversion program is terminated. If
      'Y', the conversion process goes ahead, and the file is overwritten.

Mn    This parameter defines the maximum size of a memo field. If a memo
      field is larger than this, the extra characters are not converted and
      the memo is truncated. The default for this option is 512 bytes.

      Note that memo fields are converted to null-terminated strings with
      this maximum size, and are always placed last in the converted B-Tree
      Filer record.

      The values for this option lie between 0 bytes (ie no conversion of
      memo fields will take place) and 65000 bytes. Note again that a
      single Filer record cannot grow any larger than 65527 bytes; if you
      attempt to convert a dBase record (with memo fields) that is larger
      that this, an error message will be generated.

S     This parameter defines whether dBase character fields of length
      greater than 1 should be converted to null-terminated (C style) or
      length byte strings (Pascal style). The default is S+, which forces
      character fields to be converted to Pascal style strings.

      Note that memo fields are always converted to null-terminated
      strings.

T     This parameter defines whether a Pascal include file containing a
      record type definition should be created. The default is T+, and an
      include file will be created before the actual conversion takes
      place. Set this option off (T-) to suppress the writing of this file.

A     This parameter defines whether an automatically incremented field is
      included in the B-Tree Filer record. The default is A-, which means
      that the additional field is not created. If the parameter is set to
      A+, then an additional field is inserted into the Filer record, just
      after the deletion marker field. The first record to be converted
      will have this new field set to 1, the second record will have this
      field equal to 2, and so on.


Running DB2ISAM
---------------

Before the conversion starts, the number of records in the dBase file is
shown. As the conversion progresses, the program displays the number of
records converted as a percentage.

To interrupt the conversion process, press any key. You are then prompted
to confirm the (possible) termination of the program:

Terminate conversion process? (Y/N):_

If you enter 'Y', the program terminates immediately after closing all
files. Note that the converted Filer file will contain a partial set of
converted records in this case. If you enter 'N', the conversion process
continues.

After the conversion has completed, the program will display a line similar
to the following:

Results: 187 records read; 171 records written

A difference between these records read and written values will be due to
records being skipped because (a) they were marked as deleted in the dBase
data file, or (b) if a data conversion error occurred for a given record.
In this latter case, the <dBase_FileName>.DMP file will contain details
about the error, and the program will display lines similar to:

Data conversion errors were found in 2 records.
The conversion error log file has the details.

On the other hand, if a severe error occurs during the execution of the
program, it will abort after displaying an error message.



3. The ISAM2DB Example Program
===========================================================================

Isam2Db, Copyright (C) ENZ EDV-Beratung GmbH, 1993
B-Tree Filer Utility

ISAM2DB is a demo program that demonstrates the use of the DBIMPEXP unit.
It converts a B-Tree Filer data file to a dBase III (or dBase IV) file.
Because B-Tree Filer fileblocks do not have any built-in field definitions,
any program that wants to convert data from a B-Tree Filer fileblock must
have the field definition compiled into the program: ISAM2DB.EXE will only
convert data from NETDEMO.EXE's ADDRESS.DAT file.


Calling Syntax
--------------

Call DB2ISAM with a command in the following syntax. (Calling the program
with no parameters or with an invalid parameter will result in a display
of DB2ISAM's calling syntax.)

Syntax:  Isam2dB /I <Filer_Filename> [/D <dBase_Filename>] {/<Option>n}

 <Filer_Filename>  name for *.DAT and *.DMP files
 <dBase_Filename>  name for *.DBF and *.DBT files
 Vn                type of dBase file to create, dBase III or dBase IV
                   version. The digit n must be 3 or 4.
 Kn                the key number by which the B-Tree Filer fileblock is
                   read. Specify K0 to read the data file in sequential
                   order, K1 to use the first index, etc.

A parameter always begins with a slash (/) or minus character (-).

I    The name for of the B-Tree Filer file is expected after this parameter.
     It will define the full data file name (by suffixing with '.DAT') and
     the index file name (by suffixing with '.IX'). If the program has not
     been written for this file (no record type definition exists) then the
     program is terminated immediately. Having a space between the /I and
     the name is optional.  If one or more data conversion errors occur, a
     conversion error log file (with extension '.DMP') will be produced
     with details about the errors (basically the record number and the
     field name). This parameter is required.

D    The family name for the dBase compatible data file and, if required,
     the memo file, is expected after this parameter. If there is no /D
     parameter is given the Filer file name is used (the /I parameter). The
     data file is created with extension '.DBF' and the memo file with
     '.DBT'.

     If the dBase data file exists already, the user is prompted whether
     to delete it:

     D:\ISAM\MYX\EXAMPLE.DBF already exists! Overwrite it? (Y/N) :_

     If the response is 'N' then the conversion program is terminated. If
     'Y', the conversion process goes ahead, and the file is overwritten.

Vn   This parameter indicates which format the dBase files should have. To
     create dBase III compatible files use V3; for dBase IV files use V4.
     The default is V3, and the program will create a dBase III format
     file.

Kn   This parameter determines the order in which the B-Tree Filer records
     are read. Specifying K0 means that the Filer records will be read in
     sequential order, as they appear in the data file. Specifying Kn,
     where n is the number of an index for the Filer fileblock, means that
     the records are read in key sequence. It is an error to specify a
     value for n that is greater than the number of indexes for the file-
     block. The default value is K0, read the records in sequential order.


Running ISAM2DB
---------------

Firstly the name of the B-Tree Filer file is compared with the expected
name, which is held internally. Remember that to convert from Filer files
to dBase files, the program has to 'know' the Filer record structure.
ISAM2DB can only convert ADDRESS.DAT from the NETDEMO.EXE example program.
If the file names do not match, the syntax display is shown and the program
terminated.

Before the conversion starts, the number of records in the B-Tree Filer
file is shown. As the conversion progresses, the program displays the
number of records converted as a percentage.

To interrupt the conversion process, press any key. You are then prompted
to confirm the (possible) termination of the program:

Terminate conversion process? (Y/N):_

If you enter 'Y', the program terminates immediately after closing all
files. Note that the converted dBase file will contain a partial set of
converted records in this case. If you enter 'N', the conversion process
continues.

After the conversion has completed, the program will display a line similar
to the following:

Results: 187 records read; 171 records written

A difference between these records read and written values will be due to
records being skipped because (a) they were marked as deleted in the Filer
data file, or (b) if a data conversion error occurred for a given record.
In this latter case, the <Filer_FileName>.DMP file will contain details
about the error, and the program will display lines similar to:

Data conversion errors were found in 2 records.
The conversion error log file has the details.

On the other hand, if a severe error occurs during the execution of the
program, it will abort after displaying an error message.
